l_debt":7365000.0,"debt_to_equity":3.181,"current_ratio":14.156,"free_cashflow":-67344128.0,"return_on_equity":-0.54755,"enterprise_value":182588464.0,"enterprise_to_ebitda":-1.879,"shares_outstanding":78521634.0,"float_shares":47327344.0,"held_pct_insiders":0.00604,"held_pct_institutions":1.14285,"beta":1.427,"fifty_two_week_high":10.99,"fifty_two_week_low":3.6,"fifty_day_average":5.1203,"two_hundred_day_avg":7.1286,"target_high_price":30.0,"target_low_price":9.0,"target_mean_price":20.6,"recommendation":"strong_buy","num_analyst_opinions":10,"all_time_high":22.33,"all_time_low":1.45,"long_business_summary":"Lexeo Therapeutics, Inc., a clinical stage genetic medicine company, focuses on hereditary and acquired diseases with high unmet need in the United States. Its product pipeline comprises LX2006, an AAVrh10-based gene therapy candidate, which is in phase 1/2 clinical trial to treat friedreich ataxia cardiomyopathy; LX2020, an AAVrh10-based gene therapy candidate, which is in phase 1/2 to deliver a functional plakophilin-2 (PKP2) gene to cardiac muscle for the treatment of PKP2-ACM; and LX2021, a gene therapy candidate, which is in preclinical trial to deliver the coding sequence for the functional connexin 43, or Cx43, protein for a group of inherited cardiac muscle disorders associated with a high risk of sudden death, including arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy (ACM) and certain forms of dilated cardiomyopathy. The company is also developing LX2022, a gene therapy candidate, which is in preclinical trial to deliver a functional TNNI3 gene to myocardial cells to treat a distinct form of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. In addition, it is developing LX1001, LX1020, and LX1021, an AAVrh10-based gene therapy candidate, which has completed phase 1/2 clinical trial for the treatment of APOE4-associated Alzheimer's disease. The company has a license agreement with Cornell University to conduct the Phase 1/2 clinical trial of LX1001, as well as support the development of the LX1004 program; a research collaboration agreement with Cornell University to conduct preclinical research to develop the licensed technology; and third license agreement which obtained certain rights for FA cardiomyopathy, including rights to current and future clinical data from an ongoing Cornell University investigator-initiated Phase 1A trial of a gene therapy candidate. The company was founded in 2017 and is headquartered in New York, New York."}}